# Routh I.Art.400 — a double star's period depends only on the SUM of the masses
# — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "A Treatise on Dynamics of a Particle" (Cambridge, 1898),
# Part I, Art. 400:
#
#   "the periodic time of a double star does not depend on the mass of either
#    constituent, but on the sum of the masses. The time in the same orbit is
#    the same for the same total mass however that mass is distributed over the
#    two bodies."
#
# The three binaries below share one total mass (M = 2) and one relative orbit
# (circular, separation r = 1). They differ ONLY in mass ratio: 1:1, 3:1, 19:1.
# With G = 1 the relative speed is sqrt(G*M/r) = sqrt(2), so Routh's period is
#
#     T = 2*pi*sqrt(r^3/(G*M)) = pi*sqrt(2) = 4.442882938158366
#
# Each case is run for exactly T and must return its bodies to their starting
# points — the printed residuals are ~1e-11. The CONTROL at the end keeps the
# 19:1 ratio but raises the total mass to 2.5: pi*sqrt(2) then misses by about a
# quarter of an orbit, and only that system's own period 2*pi/sqrt(2.5) closes
# it. The sum matters; the ratio does not.
#
# softening MUST be 0 here. Any epsilon breaks the exact 1/r^2 law, and by
# Bertrand's theorem (I.Art.428) the orbit would then fail to close at all.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: the scene loads the most lopsided case, 19:1 — a nearly
# stationary primary at the centre of mass with a light companion sweeping a
# circle of radius 0.95 — running at the SAME period as the symmetric 1:1
# binary proved above. One orbit takes about 8 wall-seconds.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/nsh/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_double_star_period
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_double_star_period.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_double_star_period.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---- case A : mass ratio 1:1  (mu1 = mu2 = 0.5)
set system.g_constant = 1
set system.softening = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new point { mass = 1.0, position = [-0.5, 0, 0], velocity = [0, -0.7071067811865476, 0] }
new point { mass = 1.0, position = [ 0.5, 0, 0], velocity = [0,  0.7071067811865476, 0] }
run pi * sqrt(2) steps 1
norm(obj1.position - [0.5, 0, 0])
reset

# ---- case B : mass ratio 3:1  (mu2 = 0.25, mu1 = 0.75)
set system.g_constant = 1
set system.softening = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new point { mass = 1.5, position = [-0.25, 0, 0], velocity = [0, -0.35355339059327373, 0] }
new point { mass = 0.5, position = [ 0.75, 0, 0], velocity = [0,  1.0606601717798212, 0] }
run pi * sqrt(2) steps 1
norm(obj1.position - [0.75, 0, 0])
reset

# ---- case C : mass ratio 19:1  (mu2 = 0.05, mu1 = 0.95)
set system.g_constant = 1
set system.softening = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new point { mass = 1.9, position = [-0.05, 0, 0], velocity = [0, -0.07071067811865475, 0] }
new point { mass = 0.1, position = [ 0.95, 0, 0], velocity = [0,  1.3435028842544403, 0] }
run pi * sqrt(2) steps 1
norm(obj1.position - [0.95, 0, 0])
reset

# ---- CONTROL : same 19:1 ratio, but total mass 2.5 instead of 2.
#      Routh's T depends on the sum, so pi*sqrt(2) must now FAIL to close.
set system.g_constant = 1
set system.softening = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new point { mass = 2.4, position = [-0.04, 0, 0], velocity = [0, -0.06324555320336758, 0] }
new point { mass = 0.1, position = [ 0.96, 0, 0], velocity = [0,  1.517893276880822, 0] }
run pi * sqrt(2) steps 1
norm(obj1.position - [0.96, 0, 0])
reset

# ---- the SAME control system, run instead for its own period 2*pi/sqrt(2.5)
set system.g_constant = 1
set system.softening = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new point { mass = 2.4, position = [-0.04, 0, 0], velocity = [0, -0.06324555320336758, 0] }
new point { mass = 0.1, position = [ 0.96, 0, 0], velocity = [0,  1.517893276880822, 0] }
run 2 * pi / sqrt(2.5) steps 1
norm(obj1.position - [0.96, 0, 0])
reset

# ---- the scene: case C (19:1), rebuilt at t = 0 and left Stopped
set system.g_constant = 1
set system.softening = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new point { mass = 1.9, position = [-0.05, 0, 0], velocity = [0, -0.07071067811865475, 0] }
new point { mass = 0.1, position = [ 0.95, 0, 0], velocity = [0,  1.3435028842544403, 0] }

# Baselines before anything moves: total energy, total momentum (the centre of
# mass is at rest, so this is exactly zero), and Routh's period.
energy
momentum
pi * sqrt(2)

scene create
scene zoom in
scene zoom in
scene zoom in
# 0.02 per tick at ~27 ticks/s -> one orbit (T = 4.4429) in about 8 wall-seconds.
scene set_time_step 0.02
